\textbf{Question}:\\
The straight line $5x+4y=0$ passes through the point of intersection of the straight lines $x+2y-10 = 0$ and $2x+y+5 = 0$.
 
\textbf{Solution}:\\
The given equations can be expressed as
\begin{align}
    \myvec{1 & 2 \\ 2 & 1}\vec{x} &= \myvec{10 \\ -5} \label{eq:system}
\end{align}

Thus, the point of intersection is
\begin{align}
    \vec{x} &= \myvec{1 & 2 \\ 2 & 1}^{-1}\myvec{10 \\ -5} \\
             &= \myvec{-\tfrac{20}{3} \\ \tfrac{25}{3}} \label{eq:intersection}
\end{align}

Now, check whether this point lies on the line
\begin{align}
    \myvec{5 & 4}\vec{x} &= 0
\end{align}

Substituting \eqref{eq:intersection},
\begin{align}
    \myvec{5 & 4}\myvec{-\tfrac{20}{3} \\[4pt]\tfrac{25}{3}}
    &= \frac{1}{3}(-100+100)=0
\end{align}

Hence, the straight line $5x+4y=0$ passes through the intersection of $x+2y-10=0$ and $2x+y+5=0$. 

